2-research-sufficiency-revision-r1/source-intake/sources/2026-W34/collectors/arxiv/runs/20260907T161121Z/raw/cs-cv.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.15163) in lane safety_security. Discovery triage only: matched=evaluation,inference_serving,language_models,multimodal_media,retrieval_embeddings,safety_security,training_methods; title_matches=safety_security; score=11. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: Visual brain decoding reconstructs visual content perceived by a person from neural measurements such as fMRI, providing a computational approach to studying how visual information is represented in the brain. Recent multimodal representations and diffusion priors have improved reconstruction realism. However, visually plausible reconstructions may contain incorrect objects, attributes, or relations because a strong generative prior can complete content not sufficiently specified by the decoded representation. Conventional reconstruction metrics mainly assess the final image and may therefore","title":"From \"What-If\" to \"What-Is\": Counterfactual Thinking-Inspired Semantic Alignment for Visual Brain Decoding"}}
