6-W34/collectors/arxiv/runs/20260907T161121Z/raw/cs-dc.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.15171) in lane inference_serving. Discovery triage only: matched=agents_tools,inference_serving,language_models,retrieval_embeddings; title_matches=inference_serving,language_models; score=10. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: Long-context LLM applications such as retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) and agentic systems often process tens of thousands of input tokens to produce short outputs, making end-to-end request latency an important serving objective. We show that the maximum number of batched tokens (MBT), which controls the token scheduling budget in vLLM, has a scheduling-pressure-dependent effect on latency. Larger token budgets can reduce latency under low scheduling pressure, while smaller budgets become preferable under higher pressure. Consequently, no single static MBT performs best across load regime","title":"P-PAS: Prefill-Pressure Adaptive Scheduling for Long-Context LLM Serving"}}
