v/runs/20260907T161121Z/raw/cs-dc.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.15531) in lane inference_serving. Discovery triage only: matched=inference_serving,language_models,memory_context; title_matches=inference_serving,language_models,memory_context; score=11. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: Low-bit quantization reduces the memory footprint and computational cost of large language model (LLM) inference. However, high-magnitude outlier weights can induce substantial quantization errors and degrade model accuracy. Outlier-aware quantization addresses this issue by retaining outliers in high precision while quantizing the remaining weights, resulting in a low-bit dense GEMM path and a high-precision sparse SpMM path. Existing implementations execute these paths in separate GPU kernels, despite their shared activations and outputs, thereby missing opportunities for intra-operator reus","title":"FlashQuant: Sparse-Dense Fusion for Memory-Efficient Outlier-Aware LLM Inference"}}
