urces/2026-W34/collectors/arxiv/runs/20260907T161121Z/raw/cs-lg.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.16201) in lane language_models. Discovery triage only: matched=evaluation,inference_serving,language_models,multimodal_media,safety_security; title_matches=language_models,multimodal_media; score=9. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: Multimodal sentiment analysis (MSA) aims to predict sentiment polarity and intensity from heterogeneous inputs such as text, audio, and vision. While large language models (LLMs) offer strong semantic priors for MSA, effectively incorporating audio and visual signals effectively remains challenging. A key challenge is that audio and visual sentiment cues evolve over different temporal scales, yet many LLM-based methods compress these signals through shallow projection or coarse pooling before fusing them with text, which can weaken cross-modal alignment and erase fine-grained affective informa","title":"Multi-Granularity Sentiment Integration for LLM-Based Multimodal Sentiment Analysis"}}
