s/2026-W34/collectors/arxiv/runs/20260907T161121Z/raw/cs-cr.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.17154) in lane evaluation. Discovery triage only: matched=developer_systems,evaluation,language_models,safety_security,training_methods; title_matches=evaluation,language_models,safety_security; score=13. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly being explored within Security Operation Centers (SOCs) to support text-heavy analytical work such as alert contextualization, incident summarization, and drafting investigative artifacts. Despite this interest, practitioners describe critical operational concerns, most notably hallucinations (plausible but incorrect outputs), opaque reasoning, and the verification effort required to safely use model-generated content in security workflows. In this paper, we present findings from semi-structured interviews with 20 SOC practitioners spanning frontli","title":"Beyond the Hype: Evaluating LLM Integration and Practical Limitations in Security Operation Centers"}}
