1/source-intake/sources/2026-W34/collectors/arxiv/runs/20260907T161121Z/raw/cs-ai.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.18521) in lane retrieval_embeddings. Discovery triage only: matched=evaluation,inference_serving,language_models,retrieval_embeddings,training_methods; title_matches=retrieval_embeddings; score=9. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: Dense-caption retrieval has recently been improved by introducing segmentation, edge maps, LLM-filtered captions, and cross-modal modules into contrastive fine-tuning. However, these methods largely inherit the same InfoNCE objective, whose optimization can prematurely saturate under a strong pre-trained initialization: on dense captions, the loss falls below 10^-3 on 80% of batches within the first epoch, while its gradient becomes numerically zero in 47% of measurements. We find that this behavior is closely related to the large number of near-duplicate captions in dense-caption benchmarks,","title":"Which Negatives Matter? Ask Your Text Encoder: Adaptive Similarity Margins for Dense-Caption Retrieval"}}
