on-r1/source-intake/sources/2026-W34/collectors/arxiv/runs/20260907T161121Z/raw/cs-ai.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.18524) in lane agents_tools. Discovery triage only: matched=agents_tools,evaluation,language_models,retrieval_embeddings,training_methods; title_matches=agents_tools,retrieval_embeddings,training_methods; score=13. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: Equipping Large Language Models (LLMs) with multi-turn tool-calling capabilities is essential for building autonomous agents. However, progress is fundamentally limited by the reliance on full-length trajectory imitation. For tasks involving multiple order-independent sub-goals, the optimal solution space forms a vast combinatorial diamond lattice. Forcing this rich topology into monolithic trajectories causes a severe topological collapse, indiscriminately penalizing valid alternative explorations and severely degrading policy diversity. To address this, we propose DART-SD (Diamond-topology A","title":"DART-SD: Diamond-topology Aware Retrieval and Tuning for Self-Distillation of Multi-Turn Tool-Calling Agents"}}
{"discovery_id":"w34-event-arxiv-2608-18534","issue_id":"2026-W34","provenance":{"obligation_ids":["weekly:current-relevance","weekly:technical-significance"],"origin":"REFERENCE_EXPANSION","parent_refs":["w34-gapfill-arxiv-triage-2608-18534"],"reason":"Create one independently screenable event-level record for fresh Discovery w34-gapfill-arxiv-triage-2608-18534 by 1:1 expansion of the accepted 369-record graph; retain source identity, Raw bindings, and obligations without Screening disposition. Inventory cross-check preserves split/duplicate/chronology semantics.","research_pass":1},"schema_version":"2.0-rc1","source":{"collector_id":"arxiv-api","collector_run_id":"arxiv-api-2026-W34-20260907T161121Z","locator":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.18534","metadata":{"arxiv_id":"2608.18534","authority_class":"DISCOVERY_SOURCE_ONLY","categories":["cs.AI","cs.IR"],"chronology_precision":"EXACT_PUBLISHED_IN_ATOM_SNAPSHOT","deterministic_prefilter_hit":true,"deterministic_score":12,"discovery_only":true,"duplicate_relationship":null,"event_level_discovery_id":"w34-event-arxiv-2608-18534","event_level_expansion":true,"fresh_expansion":"w34-screening-evidence-after-sol-discovery-r2","fresh_refresh":"w34-discovery-gapfill-after-sol-review-r1","iterative_evidence_priority":true,"lane":"evaluation","matched_term_groups":["evaluation","language_models","retrieval_embeddings","training_methods"],"primary_category":"cs.AI","provider":"arXiv","screening_decision_present":false,"selected_source_parent":"w34-gapfill-arxiv-triage-2608-18534","selection_not_performed":true,"technical_claims_accepted":false,"triage_bucket":"SEMANTIC_SHORTLIST","updated_at":"2026-08-19T04:36:01Z","window_relation_observation":"IN_WINDOW_DATE_CANDIDATE","window_utc":"[2026-08-14T22:00:00Z, 2026-08-21T22:00:00Z)"},"observed_at":"2026-09-08T00:51:52Z","published_at":"2026-08-19T04:36:01Z","raw_paths":["sources/2026-W34/execution/luna/w34-architecture-r2-research-sufficiency-revision-r1/source-intake/sources/2026-W34/collectors/arxiv/runs/20260907T161121Z/raw/cs-ai.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.18534) in lane evaluation. Discovery triage only: matched=evaluation,language_models,retrieval_embeddings,training_methods; title_matches=evaluation,retrieval_embeddings,training_methods; score=12. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: Large language models are increasingly used to support financial operations, but their apparent reasoning performance can depend on whether they receive the right evidence. In financial reconciliation, the evidence needed for diagnosis is distributed across invoices, purchase orders, approvals, allocations, payments, ledger entries, and bank activity, linked by transactional relationships rather than textual similarity. End-to-end accuracy can therefore conflate evidence access with reasoning quality. We introduce FinRCA-Bench, a deterministic synthetic benchmark of 2,250 accounts-payable-to-b","title":"FinRCA-Bench: Benchmarking Evidence Retrieval and Reasoning for Financial AI Systems"}}
{"discovery_id":"w34-event-arxiv-2608-18575","issue_id":"2026-W34","provenance":{"obligation_ids":["weekly:current-relevance","weekly:technical-significance"],"origin":"REFERENCE_EXPANSION","parent_refs":["w34-gapfill-arxiv-triage-2608-18575"],"reason":"Create one independently screenable event-level record for fresh Discovery w34-gapfill-arxiv-triage-2608-18575 by 1:1 expansion of the accepted 369-record graph; retain source identity, Raw bindings, and obligations without Screening disposition. Inventory cross-check preserves split/duplicate/chronology semantics.","research_pass":1},"schema_version":"2.0-rc1","source":{"collector_id":"arxiv-api","collector_run_id":"arxiv-api-2026-W34-20260907T161121Z","locator":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.18575","metadata":{"arxiv_id":"2608.18575","authority_class":"DISCOVERY_SOURCE_ONLY","categories":["cs.CL"],"chronology_precision":"EXACT_PUBLISHED_IN_ATOM_SNAPSHOT","deterministic_prefilter_hit":true,"deterministic_score":13,"discovery_only":true,"duplicate_relationship":null,"event_level_discovery_id":"w34-event-arxiv-2608-18575","event_level_expansion":true,"fresh_expansion":"w34-screening-evidence-after-sol-discovery-r2","fresh_refresh":"w34-discovery-gapfill-after-sol-review-r1","iterative_evidence_priority":true,"lane":"agents_tools","matched_term_groups":["agents_tools","evaluation","inference_serving","language_models","training_methods"],"primary_category":"cs.CL","provider":"arXiv","screening_decision_present":false,"selected_source_parent":"w34-gapfill-arxiv-triage-2608-18575","selection_not_performed":true,"technical_claims_accepted":false,"triage_bucket":"SEMANTIC_SHORTLIST","updated_at":"2026-08-19T06:16:09Z","window_relation_observation":"IN_WINDOW_DATE_CANDIDATE","window_utc":"[2026-08-14T22:00:00Z, 2026-08-21T22:00:00Z)"},"observed_at":"2026-09-08T00:51:52Z","published_at":"2026-08-19T06:16:09Z","raw_paths":["sources/2026-W34/execution/luna/w34-architecture-r2-research-sufficiency-revision-r1/source-intake/sources/2026-W34/collectors/arxiv/runs/20260907T161121Z/raw/cs-cl.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.18575) in lane agents_tools. Discovery triage only: matched=agents_tools,evaluation,inference_serving,language_models,training_methods; title_matches=agents_tools,language_models,training_methods; score=13. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: Large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems (MAS) often exhibit complex failure modes, which frequently cause agents to produce incorrect outcomes. This motivates the task of Agent Failure Attribution: given a failed multi-agent trajectory, identify the faulty agents and their corresponding error types. Existing approaches predominantly rely on LLMs to perform failure attribution, either through direct prompting, fine-tuning on synthetic data or complex agentic pipelines. While effective, these methods incur substantial computational overhead due to long-context processing, expensive","title":"Beyond LLM-Based Reasoning: Lightweight GNNs for Agent Failure Attribution"}}
