e/sources/2026-W34/collectors/arxiv/runs/20260907T161121Z/raw/cs-ai.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.19652) in lane agents_tools. Discovery triage only: matched=agents_tools,evaluation,language_models,memory_context,retrieval_embeddings; title_matches=agents_tools,memory_context; score=11. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: As LLM-based agents are deployed for longer and higher-stakes tasks, their memory systems continue to have crucial gaps. While existing memory benchmarks focus largely on recall-shaped tasks, we argue an effective memory system must track the evolving state of the world; as facts, constraints, and decisions are revised over a long interaction, answers must reflect the current state and not a superseded one. We define this capability as state tracking and instantiate it in StateMemBench, a benchmark of 234 multi-session scenarios spanning two conversation-length regimes. Its closed-pool grading","title":"Can Agent Memory Systems Track Evolving State?"}}
