07T161121Z/raw/cs-ai.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.20256) in lane evaluation. Discovery triage only: matched=evaluation,language_models,training_methods; title_matches=evaluation,training_methods; score=9. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: Reasoning language models trained with reinforcement learning typically operate under a fixed token budget rather than an explicitly adaptive one, which can lead to over-computation on easy problems and insufficient computation on difficult ones. We study whether a model can learn to allocate its own reasoning effort by choosing, as the first token of its response, one of three modes: \\textsc{NoThink} (answer as quickly as possible), \\textsc{Short} (brief reasoning), or \\textsc{Long} (extended reasoning). The choice is learned inside Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with no separate r","title":"Learning When to Think: Adaptive Reasoning for Test-Time Compute Allocation"}}
