e-intake/sources/2026-W34/collectors/arxiv/runs/20260907T161121Z/raw/cs-ai.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.20318) in lane agents_tools. Discovery triage only: matched=agents_tools,developer_systems,evaluation,language_models,training_methods; title_matches=agents_tools,evaluation,language_models; score=11. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: Recursive self-improvement (RSI) asks whether an AI system can improve the process that produces AI systems, so that the next system inherits the improvement. That process is the training algorithm: a better objective or update rule improves the compute\\mbox{-}capability exchange rate for every subsequent run, including the one that produces the next agent. Whether RSI is feasible therefore turns on whether an agent can design training algorithms. No benchmark isolates that ability: existing suites are won by collecting data or by tuning hyperparameters, and none tells a change to how a run is","title":"AI4AI-Bench: Benchmarking LLM Agents in Algorithmic Design for Recursive Self-Improvement"}}
