-intake/sources/2026-W34/collectors/arxiv/runs/20260907T161121Z/raw/cs-ai.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.20494) in lane agents_tools. Discovery triage only: matched=agents_tools,evaluation,inference_serving,language_models,training_methods; title_matches=agents_tools; score=9. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: Multi-agent LLM systems are an emerging networked workload whose rapid deployment raises questions about the traffic patterns they generate. Compared to conventional applications, these systems generate requests internally: a single user task can induce a structured sequence of model calls whose timing is governed by coordination logic rather than by user arrival rate. It is not clear whether classical traffic models, designed for human-driven workloads, apply to this setting. We present an empirical characterisation of LLM-call interarrival time distributions across sequential, star, and full","title":"Towards Traffic Modelling of Multi-Agent Systems: The Role of Coordination Topology"}}
