arxiv/runs/20260907T161121Z/raw/cs-cr.atom"],"source_type":"arxiv_atom_snapshot","summary_text":"Abstract-level arXiv Discovery lead (2608.21126) in lane agents_tools. Discovery triage only: matched=agents_tools,evaluation,language_models,safety_security; title_matches=agents_tools,language_models,safety_security; score=12. Shortlist status is provisional and requires Sol review; no downstream disposition is encoded. Abstract excerpt: Networked large language model (LLM) agents retrieve information from email, cloud storage, calendars, transaction platforms, and Web services to complete multistep tasks that produce persistent external effects. The same content needed for legitimate execution may also contain indirect prompt injections that redirect tool use, alter sensitive arguments, or disrupt task completion. Existing defenses mainly constrain untrusted content or individual tool calls, leaving user intent, runtime evidence, realized effects, and task completion insufficiently connected. We present TraceGrant, a security","title":"TraceGrant: A Contract-Governed Security Framework for the Task-Effect Lifecycle of Networked LLM Agents"}}
